# =============================================================================
# Provenance — GET /api/foods and GET /api/foods/{id}
#
# Story 3.2, FR-10. Every food says where its numbers came from. Two places:
#   the list  `source` per row, so your own food is distinguishable from the
#             catalogue's without being marked as worse
#   the detail `provenance` — the row's source, plus the fields that did not
#             come from it
#
# Per-field provenance is DERIVED, not stored. Measured on the real catalogue:
# all 1,933 rows carry all fifteen nutrients and none carries leucine, so there
# is no variation inside a row to store. Sixteen source columns would hold the
# same constant 29,000 times. What is stored is the row's source, because that
# is the part that will vary — see decision 0011 and 0012.
#
#   estimated  the value is absent and the engine fills it in (leucine, from
#              the category)
#   absent     the value is absent and nothing fills it in — the density score
#              renormalises over what is left and reports isEstimated
# =============================================================================

### A catalogue food — 200. source is UsdaFndds, absent empty, leucine estimated.
### 5347 is Milk, NFS; any id from the list above works.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods/5347
Accept: application/json

### The list — 200. Every row carries source; nothing else about it changed
GET {{secureHost}}/api/foods?pageSize=3
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST   /api/recipes          GET /api/recipes         GET /api/recipes/{id}
# PUT    /api/recipes/{id}     DELETE /api/recipes/{id}
# GET    /api/recipes/{id}/grade?lensId=
#
# Story 3.4, FR-12. A saved composition of foods with weights. Private, like
# your own foods, and behind the same global query filter — a recipe belongs to
# exactly one account and there is no shared catalogue of them.
#
# The nutrient profile is NOT stored. PortionAggregate (Story 1.10) sums the
# nutrients across the portions and restates them per 100 g of total weight,
# every time it is read. A stored profile would drift the moment an ingredient
# changed. The grade is then computed on that profile by the same formula that
# grades a single food — a plate of spinach and butter reads as butter, because
# 97% of its calories are butter, and no average of letters could say that.
#
# Absent stays absent all the way up: one ingredient that does not know its
# vitamin C makes the whole recipe not know it.
#
# Ingredients are in GRAMS only. "2 eggs" needs Food.Servings, which does not
# exist yet, so DisplayAmount/DisplayUnit from the architecture are not here.
#
# NOT built, and it is not an oversight: "adding a recipe to a Meal is one
# action" and "editing a recipe does not rewrite meals already logged" both
# need Meal, which is Epic 4. The second is solved on the Meal side — grams are
# frozen at logging time.
# =============================================================================

### Sign in — 200
POST {{secureHost}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Compose one — 201 + Location. 5347 is Milk, NFS; 5348 is Milk, whole
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Milk and the next thing along",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 5347, "grams": 200 },
    { "foodId": 5348, "grams": 100 }
  ]
}

### My recipes — 200, each with its ingredient count and total weight
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Accept: application/json

### One recipe — 200 with the derived profile per 100 g of the 300 g total.
### Replace 1 with the id the Location header returned.
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Accept: application/json

### Its grade — 200, computed on the aggregate like any single food
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1/grade?lensId=weight-loss
Accept: application/json

### A recipe with no ingredients — 400, the error sits on ingredients
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Nothing at all",
  "ingredients": []
}

### A food that does not exist, or is somebody else's — 400. Same answer for
### both, so this cannot be used to find out whether an id is taken
POST {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Not mine to use",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 999999, "grams": 100 }
  ]
}

### Edit it — 200, the ingredients are replaced and the profile follows
PUT {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Just the milk now",
  "ingredients": [
    { "foodId": 5347, "grams": 250 }
  ]
}

### The export carries recipes too — 200, with every ingredient and its weight
POST {{secureHost}}/api/account/export
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Delete it — 204, and its ingredients go with it
DELETE {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1

### Gone — 404
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes/1
Accept: application/json

### Recipes without a session — 401
GET {{secureHost}}/api/recipes
Accept: application/json
